{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y5QJJewEV1J3pC8AfuurJr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey aspects of this research:\n1. Cross-lingual calibration via correctness probes\n2. Four claims: Pareto frontier, Decomposition, CaCLR method, Cross-lingual orthogonality\n3. Two main models: Qwen3-235B-A22B (MoE) and Llama 4 Scout (MoE)\n4. 20 languages, targeting EACL 2027\n\nFrom MVP data:\n- Qwen2.5-7B: EN best AUROC 0.7541 at L27, zero-shot transfer works for 4/5 langs (not sw), clear Pareto tradeoff\n\n\n- Llama-3.1-8B: EN best AUROC 0.7284 at L31, zero-shot works for 5/5 langs, weak Pareto (language-specific)\n- Mean-shift mathematically inert with no AUROC change\n- CKA shows negative correlation with degradation (rho=-0.40, p=0.007 for Llama)\n- Control tasks reveal selectivity concentrated in L20-L27 with ranges from 0.02-0.262\n\nNow I'm identifying the critical unknowns that will shape the full-scale experiments.\n\nThe main questions are whether the depth-transferability tradeoff holds across larger models like Qwen and Llama Scout, whether the consistency loss in CaCLR actually improves performance, and how efficiently the linear projection approach scales from the 50-pair validation. I also need to test if projecting out difficulty helps transfer, whether CKA adds explanatory power beyond depth alone, and whether cross-lingual orthogonality patterns persist. Scaling from the 7B MVP to 235B and Scout will be critical to validate.\n\nFor the full experiments, I need to establish baseline ranges for EN probe AUROC on larger models and zero-shot transfer AUROC across languages.\n\nNow I'm defining what success looks like across three tiers. The best-case scenario would show a clear Pareto frontier on both architectures with distinct shapes, CaCLR providing genuine improvements, linear projection being highly sample-efficient with just 50 pairs, and strong CKA partial correlations that validate the geometric analysis. I'd also want clean separation between difficulty and capability factors, persistent orthogonality across languages, and a complete paper framing.\n\nThe middle ground would have a Pareto frontier that's clear on one architecture but weaker on the other, with CaCLR as a systematic negative result I can reframe meaningfully, linear projection requiring more data (100-200 pairs), and CKA showing only suggestive correlations. The decomposition would partially work with more variance in orthogonality, and the paper would focus on discovery, principled negative results, and practical recommendations.\n\nThe worst case would be noisy Pareto frontiers that don't hold up across models, both CaCLR and linear projection failing inconsistently, CKA adding nothing beyond depth, and decomposition that doesn't cleanly separate factors—with many low-resource results potentially just reflecting capacity limitations rather than genuine transfer phenomena.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the framing already accounts for conditional paths depending on whether CaCLR succeeds or fails, so the worst case needs to be where even the negative-result framing doesn't work well.\n\nLet me think through the three scenarios more systematically. Best case is everything working—CaCLR as a positive contribution with robust Pareto frontiers and strong CKA analysis. Median case is CaCLR failing but the Pareto discovery holding up with linear projection as a practical fallback and suggestive CKA findings, which the paper can pivot to as a discovery plus negative result. Worst case is when the core signals weaken on larger models, the Pareto frontier gets noisy, CaCLR completely fails, CKA shows no partial correlation, and control tasks expose widespread capacity artifacts in low-resource languages—forcing a reframe toward a methodological warning about cross-lingual probing limitations.\n\nNow I'm working through what the actual numbers might look like for the full-scale experiments on the larger models. English probe performance on the MVP was around 0.75-0.73 AUROC, so I'd expect better representations on larger models—probably 0.80-0.85 in the best case, 0.76-0.82 in the median, and 0.72-0.78 in the worst. For zero-shot transfer across the 20 languages, I'm estimating what those numbers should be. Looking at the performance metrics across different model architectures and training approaches. The Pareto tradeoff between depth and transfer performance shows a clear distinction between Qwen and Llama architectures, while CaCLR consistency loss provides modest gains in the best cases but minimal benefit otherwise. Linear projection with 50 pairs consistently outperforms zero-shot across all scenarios, and the CKA analysis reveals that representational similarity remains significant only in top-performing models even after controlling for depth.\n\nControl task selectivity is concentrated in early layers for well-performing models but becomes widespread in weaker ones, and cross-lingual orthogonality between correctness and confidence predictions stays relatively low across languages, though with notable variance in some cases. Now I'm thinking through how to frame the strongest narrative around what CaCLR actually achieves—the zero-label cross-lingual calibration story seems most compelling if the empirical results hold up.\n\nThe median case is more cautious: the distributional matching approach doesn't work as hoped, but a simpler linear projection with just 50-100 parallel examples gets you to supervised performance, which is still useful. There's also a methodological concern that about a quarter of the low-resource results might be artifacts of model capacity rather than genuine probing failures.\n\nThe weakest narrative is more of a cautionary tale—cross-lingual probing only really works reliably for higher-resource languages on certain architectures, and I need to establish a clear framework for when practitioners should trust these results and when they shouldn't.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the YAML structure. I should focus on the metrics that actually matter for each claim: the Pareto frontier needs English AUROC and zero-shot transfer rates, the decomposition work needs to show how well difficulty transfers across languages, CaCLR's failure versus linear projection's success, and orthogonality measured through cosine similarity. I'll also track CKA partial correlations and control task selectivity as cross-cutting metrics. Let me make sure the numbers are precise and that best ≥ median ≥ worst throughout.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16071,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16071},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8596d09d-1266-4597-bc3a-06523a96b7ab","uuid":"52552ad5-73a8-434c-ab43-3934238ff2f9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y5QJJewEV1J3pC8AfuurJr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"CaCLR consistency loss is a positive method contribution; Pareto frontier robust on both architectures; CKA geometric analysis holds after depth control; paper ships as method + discovery\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"MMD on probe logits successfully aligns correctness subspace cross-lingually (β > 0 helps)\"\n      - \"Large-model Pareto frontier is cleaner than 7B MVP (more structured representations)\"\n      - \"CKA partial correlation (controlling for depth) remains significant at 20-language scale\"\n      - \"Control task capacity artifacts confined to early layers + lowest-resource languages (<15% of lang-layer grid)\"\n      - \"Difficulty-capability decomposition cleanly separates: projecting out difficulty improves transfer for capability-dominated langs\"\n      - \"Cross-lingual orthogonality universally holds (cosine < 0.06)\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_probe_auroc: [0.80, 0.85]\n      zs_transfer_auroc_avg_20lang: [0.68, 0.74]\n      zs_transfer_success_rate: [0.85, 1.0]  # fraction of 20 langs with AUROC > 0.58\n      pareto_spearman_rho_strong_arch: [-0.75, -0.55]  # Qwen-type, negative = tradeoff\n      pareto_spearman_rho_weak_arch: [-0.45, -0.20]    # Llama-type\n      caclr_delta_auroc_vs_zeroshot: [0.03, 0.06]      # avg across langs, pp\n      caclr_delta_ece_vs_tempscaling: [-0.04, -0.02]   # negative = better calibration\n      linear_proj_50pairs_delta_auroc: [0.04, 0.08]\n      cka_partial_corr_controlling_depth: [0.30, 0.50]\n      cka_partial_corr_p: [0.001, 0.05]\n      selectivity_below_005_fraction: [0.05, 0.15]     # fraction of lang-layer combos that are artifacts\n      orthogonality_cosine_range: [0.01, 0.06]          # across 20 langs\n      difficulty_projection_transfer_delta: [0.02, 0.05] # improvement in capability transfer after projecting out difficulty\n    narrative_direction: \"原始 framing 完全成立。CaCLR 是正面方法贡献，Pareto frontier 是 discovery，decomposition 提供 prescriptive 指导，CKA 几何分析解释架构差异。Headline: 'CaCLR enables zero-label cross-lingual calibration, reducing non-English ECE by X% vs temperature scaling across 20 languages.' 四个 claims 均以强形式提出。\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"CaCLR consistency loss inert（β sweep 无效），但 Pareto discovery 稳固且 linear projection 是 practical solution；CKA 降级为 suggestive；paper 以 discovery + principled negative result + practical recommendation 发表\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"MMD distributional matching fails for correctness probes（conditional distribution mismatch，Zhao et al. 2019 预测成立）\"\n      - \"Pareto frontier 在至少一个架构上清晰，另一个 language-specific（与 MVP pattern 一致）\"\n      - \"CKA partial correlation 控制 depth 后变弱（marginal 或 n.s.），但 between-language correlation 成立\"\n      - \"Linear projection ~50-100 pairs 足够，是 zero-label 的实际方案\"\n      - \"Control task artifacts 集中在低资源语言，~25-30% 的 lang-layer 组合受影响——本身是方法论贡献\"\n      - \"Decomposition 定性成立但 effect size 小，需补充 ablation\"\n      - \"Orthogonality 跨语言持续但方差增大\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_probe_auroc: [0.76, 0.82]\n      zs_transfer_auroc_avg_20lang: [0.62, 0.68]\n      zs_transfer_success_rate: [0.70, 0.85]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_strong_arch: [-0.60, -0.40]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_weak_arch: [-0.25, -0.05]  # weak or absent on one arch\n      caclr_delta_auroc_vs_zeroshot: [-0.005, 0.015]  # consistency loss inert\n      caclr_delta_ece_vs_tempscaling: [-0.01, 0.01]   # no clear calibration gain\n      linear_proj_50pairs_delta_auroc: [0.02, 0.05]\n      cka_partial_corr_controlling_depth: [0.10, 0.25]\n      cka_partial_corr_p: [0.05, 0.20]                # marginal to n.s.\n      selectivity_below_005_fraction: [0.20, 0.30]\n      orthogonality_cosine_range: [0.02, 0.12]\n      difficulty_projection_transfer_delta: [0.005, 0.025]\n    narrative_direction: \"CaCLR 从方法贡献转为 principled negative result：β sweep across 3 OOM + component ablation 证明 MMD 对 correctness 子空间无效，CKA 几何分析解释 why（MMD matches marginals but not low-dim directional structure）。Pareto discovery + architecture contrast 仍是核心贡献。Headline: 'Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent Pareto frontier; distributional matching fails, but linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs matches supervised baselines.' Claims 强度调整：Claim 1 保持，Claim 2 降为 qualitative，Claim 3 改为 negative result + practical alternative，Claim 4 加 variance caveat。CKA 降级为 'suggestive correlational analysis'。Control task artifact warning 升级为独立贡献。\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"大模型上 Pareto 信号噪声大、CaCLR 和 linear projection 均不稳定、CKA 无解释力、大面积 capacity artifacts 侵蚀低资源结果；需重构为 cross-lingual probing 方法论批判 + viability boundary 刻画\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"大模型（235B MoE / Scout MoE）的 correctness signal 比 7B dense 更分散，probe AUROC 提升有限或 noisy\"\n      - \"Pareto frontier 在两个架构上均 noisy（rho 接近零或 language-specific 方向不一致），无法做 clean architecture contrast\"\n      - \"CaCLR 失败且 linear projection 在低/中资源语言不稳定（high variance across random seeds / pair selection）\"\n      - \"CKA partial correlation 控制 depth 后完全 n.s.——深度是唯一预测因子\"\n      - \"Control task 揭示 >30% 的 lang-layer 组合是 capacity artifacts，低资源语言几乎全部受影响\"\n      - \"Decomposition effect 被 noise dominate，无法区分 difficulty vs capability\"\n      - \"Orthogonality 在部分语言 cosine > 0.15，不再 universal\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_probe_auroc: [0.72, 0.78]\n      zs_transfer_auroc_avg_20lang: [0.56, 0.63]\n      zs_transfer_success_rate: [0.50, 0.70]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_strong_arch: [-0.35, -0.10]  # noisy, barely significant\n      pareto_spearman_rho_weak_arch: [-0.15, 0.10]     # no tradeoff or reversed\n      caclr_delta_auroc_vs_zeroshot: [-0.01, 0.005]\n      caclr_delta_ece_vs_tempscaling: [-0.005, 0.02]   # possibly worse\n      linear_proj_50pairs_delta_auroc: [0.00, 0.03]    # inconsistent, high variance\n      cka_partial_corr_controlling_depth: [-0.05, 0.12]\n      cka_partial_corr_p: [0.25, 0.80]                 # clearly n.s.\n      selectivity_below_005_fraction: [0.30, 0.50]     # widespread artifacts\n      orthogonality_cosine_range: [0.03, 0.20]         # not universal\n      difficulty_projection_transfer_delta: [-0.01, 0.015]  # noise-dominated\n    narrative_direction: \"核心 reframe：从 'cross-lingual calibration method' 转为 'cross-lingual probing viability boundary + methodological warning'。贡献重构：(1) 首个系统性压力测试证明 probe-based calibration 仅在 high-resource 语言 + 特定架构上可行——为领域划定 viability boundary；(2) Control task 分析揭示跨语言 probing 的 capacity artifact 问题（~35% false positive rate），这是对 Hewitt & Liang 框架在多语言场景下的重要扩展；(3) 方法论框架（control tasks + CKA + Pareto analysis）供后续工作评估跨语言 probing 可信度。Headline: 'Stress-testing probe-based calibration beyond English: viability boundaries, capacity artifacts, and when cross-lingual transfer breaks down.' 目标 venue 不降级（EACL）——methodological contribution + negative results + viability characterization 对该 venue 足够，但需增加 discussion section 篇幅，并可能需要补充 few-shot escalation 实验作为 constructive path forward。\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16071,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16071},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8596d09d-1266-4597-bc3a-06523a96b7ab","uuid":"4f4a5adc-73aa-4a07-92f5-b1a5631e6dde"}
